pr-worktree-rm-merged-closed [--dry-run|-n] [--yes|-y]

Clean up stale git worktrees via an fzf picker, for the current repo or
for every repo under the current directory. The mode is auto-detected:

  * Inside a git repo -> single-repo mode: operates on that repo only.
  * Otherwise, if the cwd holds primary git repos (child dirs with a
    `.git` directory, i.e. not worktree checkouts) -> multi-repo mode:
    gathers worktrees across all of them.

ONE fzf multi-select picker lists worktrees across every repo in scope
(except each repo's main worktree and, in single-repo mode, the current
one). The dir-name prefix identifies which repo each row belongs to.
Every row is flagged with its deletion category:

  [merged#N] / [closed#N]   branch has a GitHub PR in that state; any
                            leftover changes are stale merge/rebase debris.
  [no-pr/no-diff]           no PR and no commits ahead of the default
                            branch; created but never worked on.
  [open#N]                  open PR -- has active work; deletable but flagged.
  [has-work]                no PR but commits ahead of the default branch.

COLLECTIONS. pr-worktree's multi-repo mode creates a whole SET of sibling
worktrees -- one per repo -- inside a single collection dir next to the
repos' parent, e.g.

  ~/git/work/StorytelDataPlatform_20260727_DATA-2770-foo/
    data-platform-dbt/  data-platform-infra/  ...

`git worktree list` reports those wherever they live, so they always
showed up here, but as raw absolute paths and only one row at a time.
They now render relative to the repos' grandparent, and each fully
in-scope collection gets ONE aggregate row for the whole set:

  [7 wt: 5 merged, 2 open]  StorytelDataPlatform_20260727_DATA-2770-foo/
  [merged#123]                └ data-platform-dbt
  [open#456]                  └ data-platform-infra

Selecting the aggregate deletes EVERY worktree in it -- whatever their
individual states; the flag summary is there so you see what you are
dropping -- and then `rmdir`s the collection dir. rmdir, not `rm -rf`: if
a removal failed or the dir holds anything else, it refuses and the dir
is kept. Members remain individually selectable; picking both an
aggregate and one of its members deletes that member once. An aggregate
counts as deletable (and so sorts to the top, taking its members with
it) only when every member is deletable.

An aggregate appears only when the run covers the collection in full. In
single-repo mode you see just one of its worktrees while the dir still
holds the other repos', so no aggregate is offered and the worktree is
listed as an ordinary row.

Deletable rows ([merged]/[closed]/[no-pr/no-diff]) sort to the top. TAB
multi-selects, ENTER deletes the selection (after a y/N confirm), Ctrl-G
aborts (deletes nothing). The preview pane is the shared ~/bin/wt-preview
(aggregate rows preview their member list instead).

After the picker (or in --dry-run), a combined table of the REMAINING
worktrees across all repos is printed showing how stale each is:

  REPO  WORKTREE  BRANCH  BEHIND  AHEAD  AGE  CREATED

BEHIND/AHEAD are commits vs the repo's default branch (origin/HEAD,
fallback main; origin is fetched first). AGE is the last-commit date on
the branch; CREATED is the worktree directory birthtime. The main
worktree appears as a reference row labeled (main).

Each worktree is removed with `git worktree remove`, retrying with
`--force` on failure; `git worktree prune` runs once per affected repo.

Flags:
  --dry-run, -n   Skip the picker; print the deletable set and the
                  combined remaining-worktrees table. Deletes nothing.
  --yes, -y       Accepted for backwards compatibility but ignored (the
                  picker is inherently interactive).

Requires: gh, git, fzf, jq.
